    // index
    "ix.0": "Grade 4 · Texas History · §113.15 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "1835 — What Should Our Family Do?",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into a Texas colony in 1835, take on a stakeholder’s point of view, and work a real, ill-structured question — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> The year is 1835. Your family lives in a Texas colony. Far away in Mexico City, the government has changed the rules, and your neighbors are arguing — some want to stay loyal, some want reforms, some want to fight for independence. As your family, what should you do, and why?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Vocabulary, a jigsaw read on the road to 1835, and Texas regions map work. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect & organize",
    "ix.9": "Cause–effect concept map, primary sources, points of view, and a structured claim. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
    "ix.11": "Meet the problem, take a role, investigate, propose & defend a solution, debrief. ~2–4 periods.",
    "ix.12": "⛔ <b>Gate:</b> don’t open the Phase 3 problem until students have finished the surface and deep activities. Meeting the problem too early turns inquiry into guessing.",
    "ix.13": "Big idea & objectives",
    "ix.14": "<b>Big idea:</b> Real people weighed <b>loyalty, rights, risk, and community</b> when Texas moved toward revolution. Understanding their choices means seeing the problem from many sides — Anglo colonists, Tejanos, and Mexican officials all had something at stake.",
    "ix.15": "<b>Know:</b> how Spain, Mexico, and colonists shaped Texas; why tensions rose after 1830; and the causes and key events of the Texas Revolution.",
    "ix.16": "<b>Understand:</b> the same event looks different from each stakeholder’s point of view.",
    "ix.17": "<b>Do:</b> run the problem-solving process end to end — define a problem, gather information, weigh options, choose and defend a solution, and evaluate it (§113.15(c)(22)(B)).",
    "ix.18": "Standards this unit is aligned to (§113.15)",
    "ix.19": "TEKS SE",
    "ix.20": "Where it lives in the unit",
    "ix.21": "Spanish missions, colonization & motivations — Surface jigsaw",
    "ix.22": "Causes, events & effects of the Texas Revolution — Surface, Deep, Transfer",
    "ix.23": "Contributions of individuals & founders — Deep sources, Transfer roles",
    "ix.24": "Physical regions & settlement patterns — Surface map work",
    "ix.25": "Spanish colonial vs. early Mexican government — Deep concept map",
    "ix.26": "Purpose of the Texas Declaration of Independence — Deep sources, Transfer debrief",
    "ix.27": "Civic participation — Transfer solution & debrief",
    "ix.28": "Source analysis, points of view, claim + evidence — Deep & Transfer",
    "ix.29": "<b>The problem-solving process — the entire Transfer phase</b>",
    "ix.30": "Teacher prep & materials",
    "ix.31": "Print the <a href=\"facilitator-guide.html\">facilitator guide</a> (teacher moves, map of possibilities, role cards, debrief prompts) and the <a href=\"assessment.html\">assessment pack</a> (rubric + individual-in-group).",
    "ix.32": "Chart paper or a projected space for three shared columns: <b>Hunches · Know · Need-to-Know</b> (the KWHL chart).",
    "ix.33": "Vetted sources for investigation (district-approved). Suggested public-domain Texas-history sources are listed in the facilitator guide.",
    "ix.34": "No logins, no accounts — every page runs in the browser. Student writing and products live on paper or in tools your district already vets.",
    "ix.35": "📝 <b>A note on hard history:</b> slavery and racial history are part of this era. Handle them briefly, factually, and age-appropriately, and keep the family’s decision centered on <b>rights, loyalty, land, and community</b>. Guidance is in the facilitator guide.",
    "ix.36": "▶ Start Phase 1 — Surface",
    "ix.37": "🧑‍🏫 Facilitator guide",
